Learning goals
By the end of the week, you should be able to:

take a concrete example, such as a trajectory sampled at finitely many times, and name separately the underlying system, the recorded data and the point cloud or graph built for analysis;
use examples to explain which aspects of shape are geometric, such as distance and curvature, and which are topological, such as connected components and loops;
state what a homeomorphism is, beyond the rubber-sheet metaphor;
explain why a finite set of sampled points has no loop by itself, and why a finite representation must be constructed before homology can detect one; and
identify how preprocessing, metric, representation and scale choices can change a TDA result, then state what that result permits you to claim about the original system.

First baseline
Start with summaries that a domain audience already understands:

coordinate means and variances;
covariance or correlation;
a scatter plot or phase portrait;
autocorrelation or a spectral summary when temporal order matters.

Topology should complement coordinate moments, covariance, phase portraits and temporal summaries only if the scientific question concerns spatial organisation that those baselines do not retain.

Sensitivity checks
At minimum, vary one choice at a time:

rescale coordinates or use a scientifically justified metric;
change sampling density or add measurement noise;
change the neighbourhood threshold;
for delay coordinates, change the lag and embedding dimension; and
compare with a surrogate that preserves the relevant simpler structure.

Learning goals
By the end of this topic, you should be able to:

explain what repeated persistence fails to retain;
distinguish tracking observations from tracking topological classes;
choose among correspondence-based tracking, zigzag persistence, multiparameter persistence and vineyards by naming the required objects and maps;
explain the assumptions and interpretation cost added by each choice; and
recognise vineyard modules as an expert extension rather than a default method for dynamic data.

What can be skipped
For the general MasterClass audience, retain the method-selection logic and the transient zigzag example. The following are expert-level developments:

multiparameter classification and algorithms;
vineyard update algorithms;
coherent inter-module transport maps;
vineyard modules and indecomposability; and
paper-specific matrix decompositions.

Skipping those details does not interrupt the main course argument.

